# GUARD tab-label-fits-its-box
#
# The app handed the renderer the WHOLE name and let the renderer cut it, so
# the string the app believed it had painted and the string on the glass were
# two different strings. That is why a strip painting "Termi..." seven times
# survived: the widget tree, the accessibility tree and `native automate
# snapshot` all reported "Terminal 10", and only a GPU capture disagreed.
#
# The break is the call site as it shipped -- `title` instead of
# `paintedTabLabel(...)`. The test then fails on "the string fits the frame the
# layout gave it", which is the assertion the old strip could never have
# passed, at two tabs with real OSC 0 titles in the app's own minimum window.
test: the strip hands the renderer a label that already fits, so the snapshot cannot lie
red: 2026-08-15 at 83b55af
diff --git a/src/cockpit/native/view.zig b/src/cockpit/native/view.zig
index 3c97939..f3959c5 100644
--- a/src/cockpit/native/view.zig
+++ b/src/cockpit/native/view.zig
@@ -511,7 +511,7 @@ fn terminalTabTrigger(ui: *TerminalUi, model: *const Model, ws: *const Workspace
                 .wrap = false,
                 .overflow = .ellipsis,
                 .style = .{ .foreground = if (selected) tokens.colors.text else tokens.colors.text_muted },
-            }, paintedTabLabel(ui, tokens, title, projection.tabLabelWidth(extent))),
+            }, title),
             close_node,
         }),
         // The unselected bar is painted in the tab's OWN ground rather than
